  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 116 AM EST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\nChanges are on the way. Frontal shower band extends from se MN to \ncentral upper MI. This front and associated rain will cross northern \nMI later today. Much cooler air enters the region by tonight, and \nlingers thru the week. As nice as it's been (and still is), by \nMonday afternoon lake effect snow is the primary concern.\n\nShowers will push into western parts of the area (far western \nChip/Mack Cos initially) by late morning. Widespread showers reach \nTVC-PLN-ANJ by early afternoon, and GLadwin-APN by late afternoon. \nShower band will become somewhat less organized by that time. \nMeanwhile, rain will be exiting western areas by late in the \nafternoon, if only briefly. Showers will regenerate in the evening \nas overlake instability develops, along with a potent vort max \npivoting across northern areas. Coverage here maximizes in nw flow \nareas in the late evening and early overnight, before post-wave mid-\nlevel subsidence/drying moves in late in the overnight. Precip in \nthe evening will still be liquid, but overnight whatever is left \nwill turn to snow in the eastern UP and in the high terrain of \nnorthern lower. Any accums should be minimal, with near or above \nfreezing surface temps, and precip coverage decreasing very late.\n\nMonday/Mon night...850mb temps on the upstream lakeshores will be \ntumbling toward and past -10C during the day. Our airmass is \ninitially dry, and instability somewhat shallow. But a vigorous \nkicker shortwave digs in overhead in the afternoon. That will \nprovide deeper moisture and forcing, though for a relatively short \nwindow. By evening, the wave is to our east, and we back to mid-\nlevel subsidence/drying. Delta Ts exceed 20C, especially on \nnorthern Lake MI, where surface temps are an unseasonably warm +11C. \nBut the very, very warm lakes are going to try to keep near-surface \nair temps at and above freezing, and being in the peak of the \ndiurnal heating cycle will not help either. SLRs will have to be \nrelatively low, in the 10-12 range even in favored areas. Like\nother offices in the region, have lowered guidance SLRs. 1000-850mb\nwinds start out nw/wnw, veering a bit late in the day closer to\nnnw. Do have snow accums in the 1-2\" range over some parts of \nthe higher terrain of northern lower MI. Less than that \nelsewhere, including in the eastern upper MI.\n\nUpper troffing departs east, and shortwave ridging builds in by \nTuesday night. Surface high pressure will be crossing the central \nlakes w to e during this period, quickly quieting down our wx. After \nthat, uncertainties remain late this week, including the holiday \nperiod. The big wx maker in the east will miss us, a southern stream \nsystem that amplifies as it moves up the east coast late this week. \nBut a trailing, and weakening, upper low, is forecast to cross the \nnorthern lakes. A chance for precip will work north into our area on \nThanksgiving Day and evening, either as rain or a rain/snow mix. \nSmaller chances for precip after that, into next weekend.\n\nAfter another run at a few record highs today, our coldest temps of \nthe week are expected Mon-Tue-Wed. A bit of moderation after that.\nRecord highs today are below; HTL/APN are the ones most in \ndanger, and also it was pretty warm 14 years ago:\n\n ANJ 65 (2012)\n PLN 68 (2012) \n GLR 63 (2012)\n APN 62 (2012)\n TVC 65 (2012)\n HTL 60 (2012)",
